  "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 6\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[20, 18, 21], [25, 10, 8], [9, 10, 18], [6, 22, 19], [18, 7, 20], [25, 27, 18]]\n    machine_assignments = [[0, 1, 2], [1, 2, 0], [1, 0, 2], [1, 0, 2], [0, 2, 1], [0, 2, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
  "LLM_description": "To: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Coordinated schedule for current job set across specialized workstations\n\nOverview\nWe operate a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. The current production batch consists of multiple customer orders, each requiring a defined series of operations that must run in a fixed order across specific machines. Processing times for these operations are known in advance and vary by routing step and machine, reflecting the differing complexity of tasks.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe core challenge is to build a start/finish timeline for every operation so that all orders move through their required steps without conflict, and the entire batch completes as early as possible. Because several jobs require time on the same specialized machine, choices made early in the plan influence downstream waiting, idle time, and overall completion. The variation in operation durations adds to the complexity: short tasks and long tasks must be interleaved carefully to avoid bottlenecks and backlogs at shared machines.\n\nOperating Requirements\n- Each job follows a fixed sequence of operations, and later steps cannot begin until the prior step in the same job has finished.\n- Each operation is assigned to a specific machine and must be processed on that machine for its full known duration.\n- Each machine can work on only one operation at a time; no overlap or multitasking is allowed.\n- Once an operation starts on its assigned machine, it must run uninterrupted to completion (no preemption or mid-process transfers).\n- There is no parallelization of a single operation across multiple machines, and no operation can be split.\n\nTarget Outcome\nOur goal is a realistic, conflict-free production schedule that sequences all operations on their required machines while honoring the job-by-job order of steps, with the shortest possible overall completion time for the full set of jobs. A clear, machine-by-machine timeline (e.g., a Gantt view) will confirm that:\n- No machine processes more than one operation at any moment.\n- Each job\u2019s operations appear in their prescribed order without gaps that violate precedence.\n- Start times are chosen to minimize the final completion time across all jobs.\n\nNext Steps\nUsing the provided routing and time details, we will construct and validate a detailed schedule that balances machine contention, keeps machines productively utilized, and pulls the overall completion date forward as much as operationally feasible. Any constraints or updates (e.g., machine availability windows or emergent priorities) should be communicated promptly so they can be incorporated before finalizing the plan.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 6\n- Number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Job-by-job production schedule (operations must be performed in the listed sequence):\n  Job 4: Op1 on Machine 1 for 6 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 22 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 19 time units\n  Job 1: Op1 on Machine 0 for 20 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 1 for 18 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 21 time units\n  Job 3: Op1 on Machine 1 for 9 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 0 for 10 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 2 for 18 time units\n  Job 2: Op1 on Machine 1 for 25 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 10 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 0 for 8 time units\n  Job 6: Op1 on Machine 0 for 25 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 27 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 18 time units\n  Job 5: Op1 on Machine 0 for 18 time units \u2192 Op2 on Machine 2 for 7 time units \u2192 Op3 on Machine 1 for 20 time units",
